  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "It's unsettling to see your mobile home walls or ceilings start to sag, isn't it? Often, this isn't just a cosmetic issue but a sign of something deeper, like water intrusion or framing problems. Before you jump into patching, you'll want to figure out the root cause. Start by looking for water stains or any signs of leaks, especially near windows, vents, or the roofline. Sometimes, the original paneling can simply fail over time, but it's always best to rule out structural issues first. If you spot active leaks or significant sagging, it's crucial to address those before any cosmetic fixes. Covering up an active leak without fixing it is a common mistake that just leads to bigger problems down the road. Once you've identified the issue, you can decide if a simple patch will do or if you need to replace larger sections of the wall or ceiling. For anything that looks like serious structural damage or extensive water issues, it's definitely time to get a professional involved."
    }
  ],
